this is the consumer price index it's at  the highest point in 41 years  to bring it down the federal reserve  pushed through the biggest rate hike  since 1994 in june and is expected to  keep raising rates at the fastest pace  in a generation why is the fed going  against the slow and steady pace that  they've said is best for markets and for  everyday americans to put it simply  they've fallen behind on reining in  inflation i'm going to break down the  events that got us here explain how the  fed's past decisions and some outside  factors led to its current struggle and  detail what the fed is doing now that it  acknowledges being behind on inflation  let's get into it  to really understand how the fed fell so  far behind on inflation you've got to  understand the u.s central bank's  history the federal reserve was created  in 1913 to control price stability  meaning keep inflation in check but in  1978 congress gave the fed a dual  mandate not only did they need to hold  down inflation they were also mandated  to keep unemployment low fast forward to  2009 to combat the global financial  crisis not only did the fed lower u.s  interest rates to zero they begin a new  strategy of pumping trillions of dollars  into the economy by buying government  bonds when this idea was first proposed  many economists worried it would create  massive inflation but that didn't really  happen well except for asset prices  housing prices stock prices and bond  prices rose exponentially but not many  people complained about that in 2017  with rates still near zero president  donald trump signed a massive tax cut  and stimulus bill that pushed even more  money into the economy and drove the  unemployment rate to new historic lows  and even that didn't raise inflation in  fact economists at the fed started to  worry that inflation was too low in 2020  chairman jerome powell decided to try  something new he called it average  inflation targeting or ait ait just  meant the fed would keep interest rates  low until inflation rose above its 2  target and held above it for some time  since it had been below the 2 target for  some time  but ait turned out to be pretty bad  timing ait was introduced right before  the kovid 19 pandemic caused a wave of  business shutdowns the pandemic led to  the fed pushing trillions more dollars  into the economy to prop up businesses  along with trillions of dollars in  stimulus from the federal government  through tax cuts and direct payments to  americans still the fed insisted it  would stick with inflation targeting so  as inflation readings came in above the  fed's two percent target the fed not  only kept rates near zero it continued  pumping money into the economy chair  powell and the fed said the rising  prices would be transitory and that they  would fade away as supply chains  returned to normal and consumers  returned their pre-pandemic buying  patterns prices continued to rise and  the fed continued to say that they would  stop rising on their own but they didn't  and late last year the fed decided to  retire the word transitory even after  doing so the fed continued to hold rates  near zero and pump 120 billion dollars a  month into the economy they didn't stop  increasing the money supply until march  of this year when the consumer price  index had reached the highest in 40  years  while the fed and the government were  pumping money into the economy  increasing demand the world experienced  multiple shocks that reduced supply a  new coveted outbreak sprang up in china  shutting down major parts of the world  supply chain the u.s china trade war  continued to add tariffs to u.s imports  and upended long-standing business  arrangements and russia invaded ukraine  further disrupting the distribution of  food and oil higher demand plus lower  supply usually leads to higher prices  all of those things got us here and now  the fed is trying to make up for lost  time with many of its members even  saying that they are behind the curve  they started by raising interest rates  the normal pace in march then raised by  double the usual rate in may and in june  they raised rates at three times the  normal rate markets are expecting an  increase of that size in july but  interest rates are still more than seven  percentage points below the rate of  inflation and it's not clear how high  interest rates will have to go before  inflation gets back down to the fed's  target two percent some have argued the  fed is making a big mistake by raising  rates this fast  only time will tell  griselda's benny the butcher reminded us  recently that where you are isn't where  you have to be he said remember waking  up being broke i hated that feeling my  sacrifice and dedication  just made a few million  that's all for now see you next time  [Music]
 

